Setting up

projects &lt;- projects |&gt;
  mutate(opening_time = Surv(months, opened), .keep = "unused")

set.seed(403)
projects_split &lt;- initial_split(projects, prop = 0.8)
projects_train &lt;- training(projects_split)
projects_folds &lt;- vfold_cv(projects_train, v = 10)

projects_rec &lt;- recipe(opening_time ~ ., data = projects_train) |&gt;
  update_role(project, new_role = "id") |&gt;
  step_log(budget, n_operations) |&gt;
  step_other(arrondissement, theme, threshold = 0.02)

cox_spec &lt;- proportional_hazards() |&gt;
  set_engine("survival") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression")

projects_metrics &lt;- metric_set(
  brier_survival,
  brier_survival_integrated,
  concordance_survival
)

Two new engines for random forests

ranger_spec &lt;- rand_forest(trees = 500) |&gt;
  set_engine("ranger") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression")

rfsrc_spec &lt;- rand_forest(trees = 500) |&gt;
  set_engine("randomForestSRC") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression")



switching model type or engine is one line
why these? they are fast
why random forests? generally work well out of the box
but also: why tree-based models? censored has quite a few of those!
censored smooths over engine quirks, e.g. randomForestSRC’s 0/1 status coding and pre-made Surv responses in recipes
